WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.122 --> 00:00:02.725
- He's an Indian composer,
singer-songwriter

00:00:02.725 --> 00:00:04.002
and music producer.

00:00:04.002 --> 00:00:06.247
In a notable two-decade long plus career,

00:00:06.247 --> 00:00:08.320
he's been credited with redefining

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.389
the contemporary Indian
music film industry.

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:13.978
His body of work for film
and stage has earned him

00:00:13.978 --> 00:00:16.797
the nickname as the
Mozart of his industry.

00:00:16.797 --> 00:00:18.038
He's A.R. Rahman

00:00:18.038 --> 00:00:21.085
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:21.085 --> 00:00:22.602
Rule number three is my personal favorite,

00:00:22.602 --> 00:00:25.065
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:25.065 --> 00:00:26.336
And as always, guys, if you hear something

00:00:26.336 --> 00:00:28.093
that really resonates with you,

00:00:28.093 --> 00:00:29.891
please leave it down the comments below,

00:00:29.891 --> 00:00:31.111
put quotes around them as well

00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:32.571
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:33.840
Also, when you write it down

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.915
you're much more likely to lock
it in for yourself as well.

00:00:36.915 --> 00:00:37.983
Enjoy.

00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:39.389
(electronic wind blowing)

00:00:39.389 --> 00:00:41.064
(electronic slamming)

00:00:41.064 --> 00:00:43.647
(upbeat music)

00:00:46.544 --> 00:00:48.711
(beeping)

00:00:49.827 --> 00:00:50.724
(electronic wind)

00:00:50.724 --> 00:00:52.307
- What is modern is

00:00:55.201 --> 00:00:58.317
rediscovering yourself,
rediscovering the way music is done

00:00:58.317 --> 00:00:59.541
is modern.

00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:02.791
I do love to embrace new technology and

00:01:03.954 --> 00:01:04.787
new ideas.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.639
And especially like in
the film, like Slum Dog,

00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:12.622
where Danny suggested am I someone

00:01:12.622 --> 00:01:15.762
in operation of music and everything.

00:01:15.762 --> 00:01:18.538
So it was like two
cultures meeting in a way.

00:01:18.538 --> 00:01:20.435
Like some of the people
who are very close-minded

00:01:20.435 --> 00:01:23.838
say oh what are they
doing, it's just noise.

00:01:23.838 --> 00:01:26.833
Rap is noise and heavy metal is noise.

00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:30.948
So when you go into that
frame of just try to

00:01:30.948 --> 00:01:33.887
like something for what
it is, not, you know,

00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:35.939
go take a lens and

00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:38.806
disrupt it and try and analyze it.

00:01:38.806 --> 00:01:40.164
So with this philosophy, I think

00:01:40.164 --> 00:01:42.710
I kept moving on for like eight years.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.338
Rediscovering myself.

00:01:44.338 --> 00:01:46.368
Music has given me everything.

00:01:46.368 --> 00:01:48.484
Respect, Oscars, Grammys and you know,

00:01:48.484 --> 00:01:50.806
love and money and fame and everything.

00:01:50.806 --> 00:01:52.556
So I'm into it.

00:01:52.556 --> 00:01:54.831
I would rather sit in a room, do my music

00:01:54.831 --> 00:01:59.157
and pray and then sleep
and that's my kind of.

00:01:59.157 --> 00:02:01.057
But it how pushed me
to become this person,

00:02:01.057 --> 00:02:05.160
a founder of a Conservatory,
is these incidents.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.413
And after you've become 40

00:02:07.413 --> 00:02:10.423
you see that you're hitting a wall.

00:02:10.423 --> 00:02:14.223
You're seeing that you're
going towards death.

00:02:14.223 --> 00:02:18.255
Which is the question
which pushes many people to

00:02:18.255 --> 00:02:21.607
start doing many things because
of the fear of not living.

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:23.397
I mean, I didn't have a fear,

00:02:23.397 --> 00:02:25.395
but I was fearless
because of that in a way.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:28.196
So even if I fail in this attempt

00:02:28.196 --> 00:02:31.729
of doing this music
education thing, that's fine.

00:02:31.729 --> 00:02:32.562
I've tried something nice and I'd failed.

00:02:32.562 --> 00:02:33.932
(slamming)

00:02:33.932 --> 00:02:38.024
- Your success has been
dramatic, obviously

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.936
and coming from a city
like Chennai, of course

00:02:40.936 --> 00:02:45.465
culturally very rich we are
in our state of Tamil Nadu,

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:48.291
but more importantly to be
able to go global with that

00:02:48.291 --> 00:02:51.218
has been just quite
awe-inspiring to be honest.

00:02:51.218 --> 00:02:53.901
And made all of us so
proud here in Chennai.

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:56.872
But if you look at young musicians today

00:02:56.872 --> 00:02:59.624
and what they are being offered,

00:02:59.624 --> 00:03:03.047
and the technology, it's
moved along as well.

00:03:03.047 --> 00:03:04.719
What would you say to them?

00:03:04.719 --> 00:03:07.162
- I think they have to
find their own voice.

00:03:07.162 --> 00:03:08.844
They found their own inner voice.

00:03:08.844 --> 00:03:11.279
The moment we try to do somebody else's

00:03:11.279 --> 00:03:12.877
we become mediocre.

00:03:12.877 --> 00:03:14.573
We want to be try,

00:03:14.573 --> 00:03:18.737
our limitations are sometimes our plusses.

00:03:18.737 --> 00:03:22.798
So sometime that forces
you to think in a way where

00:03:22.798 --> 00:03:24.972
there are many millions
of ways to achieve a song,

00:03:24.972 --> 00:03:26.389
achieve greatness

00:03:27.425 --> 00:03:28.615
in art.

00:03:28.615 --> 00:03:29.913
So you find a different way,

00:03:29.913 --> 00:03:31.854
you don't have to,

00:03:31.854 --> 00:03:35.197
you know, a classic
example is Kumar Gandharva,

00:03:35.197 --> 00:03:36.378
the classical singer.

00:03:36.378 --> 00:03:39.201
He had a lung problem
or something like that

00:03:39.201 --> 00:03:41.326
so he couldn't sustain his notes.

00:03:41.326 --> 00:03:43.428
If you listen to his rendering,

00:03:43.428 --> 00:03:45.637
he would just make everything short.

00:03:45.637 --> 00:03:48.169
♫ Ah

00:03:48.169 --> 00:03:49.002
He wouldn't like

00:03:49.002 --> 00:03:49.999
♫ Ah.

00:03:49.999 --> 00:03:51.956
He would never sustain it
because of his breathing problem

00:03:51.956 --> 00:03:53.718
But that became a great style.

00:03:53.718 --> 00:03:56.269
You don't have to copy that
because his limitation became

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.588
a style of what he could amazingly do

00:03:59.588 --> 00:04:02.255
when it was unique when he sang.

00:04:03.213 --> 00:04:05.281
So I think all of us have limitations

00:04:05.281 --> 00:04:08.886
and we should not think it's a limitation.

00:04:08.886 --> 00:04:11.305
So there's something else which is there,

00:04:11.305 --> 00:04:12.415
a plus within you

00:04:12.415 --> 00:04:13.855
and then bring that out.

00:04:13.855 --> 00:04:15.137
And then you become totally different.

00:04:15.137 --> 00:04:16.781
- Surely in the past,

00:04:16.781 --> 00:04:19.325
you must have been criticized by people.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:22.028
And sometimes people say
some really harsh things.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:25.153
How do you deal with that criticism

00:04:25.153 --> 00:04:28.894
and what advice will you give
to other musicians out there

00:04:28.894 --> 00:04:29.894
facing that?

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:33.872
- You have to

00:04:35.554 --> 00:04:38.810
elevate yourself to be
the harshest critic.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:40.613
And when that comes

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:44.413
so you supersede every other critic.

00:04:44.413 --> 00:04:46.569
And you become a better
critic than everybody else.

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:50.455
You wouldn't be bothered because
the criticism which comes

00:04:50.455 --> 00:04:52.609
you've already done it to yourself

00:04:52.609 --> 00:04:54.729
and you can say, yeah, I
know that, I'm working on it

00:04:54.729 --> 00:04:56.095
in your mind.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:58.103
When there's a tough
situation in your life,

00:04:58.103 --> 00:04:59.800
consider this idea.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.012
Slow down your mind, detach
yourself from sadness,

00:05:03.012 --> 00:05:05.436
and always be bold to walk the path.

00:05:05.436 --> 00:05:07.741
You will certainly find relief, solutions,

00:05:07.741 --> 00:05:11.152
breakthrough ideas and
extraordinary rewards.

00:05:11.152 --> 00:05:13.494
But do not expect them soon.

00:05:13.494 --> 00:05:16.141
They will come to you at the right time

00:05:16.141 --> 00:05:18.683
because if rewards come too soon,

00:05:18.683 --> 00:05:20.222
you will not be able to handle it

00:05:20.222 --> 00:05:22.677
and it could be a recipe for disaster.

00:05:22.677 --> 00:05:26.307
- What is your advice and
motivation for young people

00:05:26.307 --> 00:05:28.691
who want to pursue a career in music?

00:05:28.691 --> 00:05:29.942
With what amount of knowledge and content

00:05:29.942 --> 00:05:31.859
does one break through?

00:05:32.882 --> 00:05:35.067
- I feel whatever,

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:37.879
first of all, you need
to have a ear for music.

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:41.052
You need to love beautiful stuff,

00:05:41.052 --> 00:05:42.767
then it becomes part of you.

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:43.600
- That's right.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.134
- It's even within spirituality
or music's the same thing,

00:05:46.134 --> 00:05:48.922
the more you start getting into something

00:05:48.922 --> 00:05:50.922
and then it becomes you.

00:05:52.871 --> 00:05:54.931
So if you're half-heartedly
doing something

00:05:54.931 --> 00:05:57.845
and you don't give enough
time, it's not worth it.

00:05:57.845 --> 00:06:01.243
And don't even think
about sustenance, nothing,

00:06:01.243 --> 00:06:03.454
and then just go and do
it become a master of it

00:06:03.454 --> 00:06:05.680
people will come following you.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.066
And that's the truth.

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:09.034
- You know, the pressure must be enormous,

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:11.681
you know, oh, he's won an Academy Award,

00:06:11.681 --> 00:06:14.964
the next time around you
were nominated for 127 Hours,

00:06:14.964 --> 00:06:18.338
of course, but I mean
what is that feeling like

00:06:18.338 --> 00:06:21.816
I mean it's like, you know,
when you win a major tournament

00:06:21.816 --> 00:06:23.227
and you come back the next year,

00:06:23.227 --> 00:06:25.142
everyone's looking to say, oh,
he's going to win it again.

00:06:25.142 --> 00:06:27.080
Which is not the case, of course.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.457
- Yeah, for me, I think I
made my mind realize that

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:34.482
you know, two is enough for your lifetime.

00:06:34.482 --> 00:06:36.759
And if anything comes it's a bonus.

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:39.331
And so your mind is not
thinking about awards,

00:06:39.331 --> 00:06:41.591
it's thinking about art.

00:06:41.591 --> 00:06:43.812
And so that's a good thing I thought,

00:06:43.812 --> 00:06:48.333
and I'm slowly also becoming
more familiar with visual stuff

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:50.152
I'm trying to create concepts.

00:06:50.152 --> 00:06:52.373
Because after a point you have a monotony.

00:06:52.373 --> 00:06:55.730
People in India, they
have a format of music

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:58.570
which is, give me five
songs, give me six songs,

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:00.567
I'll make a movie.

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:02.490
And then when you work within Broadway

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:05.014
and Western and everything, you feel like

00:07:05.014 --> 00:07:06.115
how are these guys doing?

00:07:06.115 --> 00:07:08.064
They're doing something different.

00:07:08.064 --> 00:07:10.532
They're going more deeper into

00:07:10.532 --> 00:07:14.292
what could be achieved,
the marriage of both.

00:07:14.292 --> 00:07:15.839
So that forced me to,

00:07:15.839 --> 00:07:20.183
and these long flights too,
LA from Dubai and Emirates.

00:07:20.183 --> 00:07:22.774
I'll be thinking of how do you

00:07:22.774 --> 00:07:24.524
take music to another

00:07:26.562 --> 00:07:27.972
sensible place.

00:07:27.972 --> 00:07:29.315
And it is very challenging,

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:31.439
and putting a challenge on yourself.

00:07:31.439 --> 00:07:32.619
And you're burdening yourself,

00:07:32.619 --> 00:07:34.088
but then it's totally worth it.

00:07:34.088 --> 00:07:37.907
So I'm doing music videos now, trying to.

00:07:37.907 --> 00:07:40.619
It's not an easy thing,
but it's keeping me engaged

00:07:40.619 --> 00:07:42.758
and I'm learning a lot.

00:07:42.758 --> 00:07:44.978
I cannot believe that it's over 10 years

00:07:44.978 --> 00:07:45.853
since my music and me

00:07:45.853 --> 00:07:48.666
have been associated
with Miami University.

00:07:48.666 --> 00:07:50.618
I've enjoyed collaborating
with your students,

00:07:50.618 --> 00:07:53.936
your faculty, particularly
from Team Global Rhythms

00:07:53.936 --> 00:07:55.133
and above all,

00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:58.089
every interaction has allowed
for greater understanding,

00:07:58.089 --> 00:08:00.006
sensitivity and growth.

00:08:01.178 --> 00:08:04.282
I feel humbled by the fact
that so many students here

00:08:04.282 --> 00:08:08.449
from another culture have made
my music part of their lives.

00:08:09.318 --> 00:08:11.172
Learning from this
experience has allowed me

00:08:11.172 --> 00:08:14.741
to gain considerable
confidence in starting my own

00:08:14.741 --> 00:08:17.531
Western Classical Conservatory in India.

00:08:17.531 --> 00:08:21.636
Above all, there are several
Miami students who have come

00:08:21.636 --> 00:08:24.588
over to assist me in making
KM Music Conservatory

00:08:24.588 --> 00:08:26.456
grow in multiple ways,

00:08:26.456 --> 00:08:27.906
and they have grown themselves,

00:08:27.906 --> 00:08:30.222
learning from our culture
back in South India.

00:08:30.222 --> 00:08:32.004
Learning through travel.

00:08:32.004 --> 00:08:34.672
Keep an open mind, and be
ready to receive things

00:08:34.672 --> 00:08:36.976
that come along the journey.

00:08:36.976 --> 00:08:41.349
So often, we have an opinion
from someone or a place

00:08:41.349 --> 00:08:43.912
or a culture before
heading to a destination.

00:08:43.912 --> 00:08:46.548
Each one of us are made differently.

00:08:46.548 --> 00:08:51.538
Sometimes there are many
hidden truths of a place,

00:08:51.538 --> 00:08:55.273
that never reveal to us,
that are never revealed to us

00:08:55.273 --> 00:08:56.903
'til we experience it ourselves.

00:08:56.903 --> 00:08:58.968
- What for you today is the challenge.

00:08:58.968 --> 00:09:01.998
Is it the vary of repeating yourself?

00:09:01.998 --> 00:09:03.422
- Ultimately, it's all the same.

00:09:03.422 --> 00:09:04.255
- Okay.

00:09:04.255 --> 00:09:06.941
- Something is catchy,
it reaches the public.

00:09:06.941 --> 00:09:07.774
- Right.

00:09:07.774 --> 00:09:09.521
- Something's melodic, they recognize it.

00:09:09.521 --> 00:09:13.920
But how can you give it
in different forms where

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.202
they don't feel like, oh, it's
the same thing for 20 years.

00:09:17.202 --> 00:09:18.182
- Right.

00:09:18.182 --> 00:09:20.666
- At the same time, you
know, musically explore,

00:09:20.666 --> 00:09:23.833
even take an even more dangerous path.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.784
Let's see what happens.

00:09:26.784 --> 00:09:30.951
I feel the thirst to learn
is very, very important.

00:09:32.132 --> 00:09:35.277
Even now I feel like I'm not learn enough

00:09:35.277 --> 00:09:37.853
And when certain situations
come in, I said, Oh my God,

00:09:37.853 --> 00:09:39.252
I should have learned music properly.

00:09:39.252 --> 00:09:41.678
And I should have learned
counterpoint properly.

00:09:41.678 --> 00:09:44.345
The speed is what gets affected.

00:09:45.637 --> 00:09:48.278
You can do anything, but then

00:09:48.278 --> 00:09:50.066
if you have a very strong mind and will,

00:09:50.066 --> 00:09:53.692
you can do anything, but
at what speed can you do?

00:09:53.692 --> 00:09:55.338
And also when you learn something,

00:09:55.338 --> 00:09:58.671
it also rivets you to one kind of genre,

00:09:59.712 --> 00:10:01.571
where you're specializing.

00:10:01.571 --> 00:10:03.611
For me, it was, I was always learning.

00:10:03.611 --> 00:10:06.784
So I was always moving from
thing to another thing.

00:10:06.784 --> 00:10:08.449
So whatever fascinated me,

00:10:08.449 --> 00:10:09.894
I kept moving.

00:10:09.894 --> 00:10:12.955
So my music was never
rooted to one little thing,

00:10:12.955 --> 00:10:13.788
it had a,

00:10:14.693 --> 00:10:16.790
probably people say my style,

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:18.364
but then I kept moving.

00:10:18.364 --> 00:10:20.865
(electronic wind)

00:10:20.865 --> 00:10:22.850
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.820
I made this video because
Anushka asked me to,

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:25.962
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:10:25.962 --> 00:10:27.687
that you want me to profile next,

00:10:27.687 --> 00:10:28.893
leave it down in the comments below

00:10:28.893 --> 00:10:29.906
and I will see what I can do.

00:10:29.906 --> 00:10:31.990
I'd also love to know what
did you learn from this video

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:33.556
that you're going to
immediately apply somehow

00:10:33.556 --> 00:10:35.114
to your life or your business?

00:10:35.114 --> 00:10:38.851
What was your favorite
clip of the 10, and why?

00:10:38.851 --> 00:10:40.373
Please leave it in the comments below.

00:10:40.373 --> 00:10:41.683
I'm super-curious to find out.

00:10:41.683 --> 00:10:42.901
Finally, I want to give a quick shout-out

00:10:42.901 --> 00:10:44.055
to Kalie and Bradley.

00:10:44.055 --> 00:10:46.509
Thank you guys so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:10:46.509 --> 00:10:48.870
Your One Word, and posting that picture.

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.536
I really love it, it means a lot to me,

00:10:50.536 --> 00:10:51.902
I appreciate the support,

00:10:51.902 --> 00:10:52.940
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:54.772
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:10:54.772 --> 00:10:55.879
I believe in you.

00:10:55.879 --> 00:10:57.716
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:10:57.716 --> 00:10:59.337
and whatever your one word is.

00:10:59.337 --> 00:11:00.739
Much love.

00:11:00.739 --> 00:11:02.329
I'll see you soon.

00:11:02.329 --> 00:11:06.496
(electronic wind blowing)
(electronic slamming)

00:11:08.332 --> 00:11:10.288
- Never rush into judgment.

00:11:10.288 --> 00:11:11.587
Especially about people.

00:11:11.587 --> 00:11:14.786
There always another side
of the story, a good side.

00:11:14.786 --> 00:11:17.986
We must always find ways
to see the beauty and value

00:11:17.986 --> 00:11:22.368
of a person or a place or an
institution or an art form.

00:11:22.368 --> 00:11:24.435
And half the problems of each of us

00:11:24.435 --> 00:11:26.505
can be solved if people
don't rush into judgment.

00:11:26.505 --> 00:11:28.823
It think, like in every profession,

00:11:28.823 --> 00:11:31.467
when you're first learning to drive,

00:11:31.467 --> 00:11:32.762
you're like, oh, I should
keep my clutch ready,

00:11:32.762 --> 00:11:34.225
I should put my brakes.

00:11:34.225 --> 00:11:36.302
I mean after a point of time you just

